Title: THE LAST FERRY
Credit: An original short for the Backlot demo
Draft date: 2026

FADE IN:

EXT. HARBOUR ROAD - DAWN

Fog sits on the water. MAYA (30s, oilskin jacket, a ferry captain's cap she has not earned yet) walks the empty road with a thermos and a set of keys too big for her pocket.

She stops at a padlocked gate. A hand-painted sign: LAST SAILING 6PM. EVER.

MAYA
(to nobody)
Right then.

EXT. FERRY DECK - DAY

The ferry is small, rusted, loved. Maya hoses the deck. OLD TOM (70s, cardigan, tea) watches from the wheelhouse door.

OLD TOM
You'll wear the paint off.

MAYA
There's no paint left to wear.

OLD TOM
Then you'll wear the boat off.

She keeps hosing. He keeps watching. Neither says what they are both thinking.

INT. WHEELHOUSE - DAY

Cramped. Charts, a cracked radio, a photo of a much younger Tom with a much newer ferry. Maya turns the key. The engine coughs, catches.

OLD TOM
She likes you.

MAYA
She's stalled twice this week.

OLD TOM
She liked me and she stalled on me for forty years.

INT. TICKET BOOTH - DAY

A queue that is not a queue: three people and a dog. JUNE (40s, council lanyard, clipboard) is at the front, not buying a ticket.

JUNE
I'm here about the notice.

MAYA
You put up the notice.

JUNE
I'm here about your response to the notice.

MAYA
I haven't responded.

JUNE
That's the response I'm here about.

EXT. FERRY DECK - DAY

Mid-crossing. The dog has the best seat. A SCHOOLBOY (10) leans on the rail. Maya coils rope beside him.

SCHOOLBOY
Is it true it's the last one?

MAYA
That's what the sign says.

SCHOOLBOY
Signs are wrong sometimes.

MAYA
Not council signs.

SCHOOLBOY
Especially council signs.

She almost laughs. Almost.

EXT. HARBOUR ROAD - DUSK

The gate again. June waits with two coffees. She hands one over. A truce, not a surrender.

JUNE
The bridge opens in March.

MAYA
Nobody on the island asked for a bridge.

JUNE
Nobody on the island answered the consultation.

MAYA
We were on the ferry.

INT. WHEELHOUSE - NIGHT

Maya alone. The radio hisses. She takes down the photo of young Tom, looks at it, puts it in her jacket.

She picks up the handset.

MAYA
(into radio)
Last sailing. Anyone for the last sailing.

Static. Then, faintly, a voice.

OLD TOM (V.O.)
(over radio)
Room for one more?

EXT. FERRY DECK - NIGHT

Lights on the water. Old Tom on the deck with a folding chair and a flask. The Schoolboy with his mother. June, without her clipboard. The dog.

Maya casts off. The engine catches first time.

MAYA
She likes me.

The ferry pulls out into the dark, full.

FADE OUT.
